# rich-readable

Study content (topics especially; also term `what` / Q&A answers) is written as **rich-readable** HTML, not long paragraphs.

Wrap the body in `<div class="rich-readable">`. That stamps a visible **rich-readable** mark and unlocks color roles.

## Shape

1. Keyword **chips** at the top (`.chips` / `.chip`)
2. One-line **tldr** (`.tldr`)
3. Short **bullets** (`.tight`) — split long sentences
4. **Bold** / `<span class="tag">` / `<code>` on key words
5. **Boxes**: `.scale` > `.pan`, `.note`, `.callout`
6. **Flow**: `<pre class="mermaid">` or `.flow` ASCII
7. **See also** as `<a class="chip" href="#id">` (terms) or `#topic/id`

## Color roles

- `.go` — keep / do this (green)
- `.warn` — trade-off (orange)
- `.trap` — don't (red)

```html
<div class="rich-readable">
  <div class="chips"><span class="chip">keyword</span></div>
  <div class="tldr"><b>In one line:</b> …</div>
  <div class="scale">
    <div class="pan go"><h4>Keep</h4><ul class="tight"><li>…</li></ul></div>
    <div class="pan warn"><h4>Trade-off</h4></div>
    <div class="pan trap"><h4>Trap</h4></div>
  </div>
  <pre class="mermaid">flowchart LR
    A[in] --> B[out]</pre>
</div>
```

Do not dump walls of prose. Do not invent one-off inline colors — use `.go` / `.warn` / `.trap`.
